Implement MPI_Type_create_f90_* in the standard-ABI build

Not an upstream backport, unlike the other patches this script applies: a local
fix for a defect not reported upstream yet, and worth reporting.

In an ABI build, `MPI_Type_create_f90_real`, `MPI_Type_create_f90_integer` and
`MPI_Type_create_f90_complex` all report MPI_SUCCESS and hand back
MPI_DATATYPE_NULL -- 512, or 0x200, through MPI_Type_toint. Anything done with
the result then fails with "Invalid datatype". They are C routines that MPI-5.0
requires of every implementation, so an ABI build has to have them.

The cause is a guard, not the handle conversion. `src/mpi/datatype/create_f90.c`
carries two implementations of all three routines: the real ones, and a set of
stubs that do `*newtype = MPI_DATATYPE_NULL; return MPI_SUCCESS;`. The stubs are
selected by `#ifndef HAVE_FORTRAN_BINDING`, and the standard-ABI build undefines
that macro -- `src/binding/abi/mpi_abi_internal.h`, generated by
`maint/gen_abi.py`, ends with

    typedef int MPI_Fint;
    typedef struct MPI_F08_status_dummy MPI_F08_status;
    #undef HAVE_FORTRAN_BINDING

which is right in itself, the standard ABI having no Fortran bindings, and which
every ABI object sees because `abi_cppflags` puts `src/binding/abi` on the
include path. So the ABI copy of the file gets the stubs while the ordinary
library gets the real code, in the same build:

    src/mpi/datatype/.libs/create_f90.o                     9496 bytes, has f90Types
    src/mpi/datatype/.libs/lib_libpmpi_abi_la-create_f90.o   832 bytes, three
                                                            functions at 16-byte
                                                            spacing -- the stubs

What these routines need is the Fortran data *model* and not the Fortran
*bindings*. The model is `src/include/mpif90model.h`, which configure generates
from the Fortran compiler's own `precision()` and `range()`, and which the ABI
build does not disturb. The two were conflated in one macro.

So this adds `MPIR_HAVE_F90_MODEL`, true when MPICH has Fortran bindings or when
this is the standard-ABI build, and guards on that instead. Nothing changes for a
non-ABI build. For an ABI build configured `--disable-fortran` the real code now
compiles against the fallback model configure substitutes when Fortran is off
(real 6,37; double 15,307; integer map {9,4,4}) -- defensible, and better than
reporting success while returning MPI_DATATYPE_NULL, though not a configuration
mpif builds.

Measured on mpich/gcc/darwin/26/arm64: the reproducer
`bug-mpich-f90-datatypes/mpich-abi-f90-datatype-bug.c` goes from "BROKEN (6)" to
"all ok (0 failures)", the error path comes back too --
`MPI_Type_create_f90_integer(100, &t)` now reports "No integer type with 100
digits of range is available" where it used to report success -- and five suite
tests go green: trf90, trf08, createf90, createf08 and createf90types.

`bug-mpich-f90-datatypes/mpich-abi-f90-datatype-check.c` beside it holds the fix
to more than non-nullness, since a stub that returned some *wrong* type would
satisfy the reproducer: extents 8, 4 and 16 against MPICH's own model map, the
right combiner, the (p, r) the type was created with, the empty
array_of_datatypes the standard requires of an unnamed predefined datatype, and
the `f90Types` cache handing back one handle per request.

This does not touch the nine expected failures whose symptom is the
`ABI_Datatype_from_mpi` assert in `src/binding/abi/mpi_abi_util.h`; those were
attributed to this same defect and are a separate one. Confirmed by running
f08/coll after the fix: alltoallwf08, nonblockingf08, vw_inplacef08 and
nonblocking_inpf08 still fail.

--- a/src/mpi/datatype/create_f90.c
+++ b/src/mpi/datatype/create_f90.c
@@ -6,7 +6,21 @@
 #include "mpiimpl.h"
 #include "mpif90model.h"
 
-#ifndef HAVE_FORTRAN_BINDING
+/* What these routines need is the Fortran *data model* -- mpif90model.h, which
+ * configure generates either from the Fortran compiler's own precision() and
+ * range() or, with Fortran disabled, from a set of fallback values -- and not
+ * MPICH's Fortran *bindings*. The two were conflated, and the standard-ABI build
+ * is where that becomes visible: src/binding/abi/mpi_abi_internal.h ends with
+ * `#undef HAVE_FORTRAN_BINDING`, correctly, since the standard ABI has no Fortran
+ * bindings, and that silently selected the stubs below -- which return
+ * MPI_DATATYPE_NULL and MPI_SUCCESS. MPI_Type_create_f90_{real,integer,complex}
+ * are C routines that MPI-5.0 requires of every implementation, so an ABI build
+ * has to have them for real. */
+#if defined(HAVE_FORTRAN_BINDING) || defined(BUILD_MPI_ABI)
+#define MPIR_HAVE_F90_MODEL 1
+#endif
+
+#ifndef MPIR_HAVE_F90_MODEL
 int MPIR_Type_create_f90_integer_impl(int range, MPI_Datatype * newtype)
 {
     *newtype = MPI_DATATYPE_NULL;
@@ -25,7 +39,7 @@
     return MPI_SUCCESS;
 }
 
-#else /* HAVE_FORTRAN_BINDING */
+#else /* MPIR_HAVE_F90_MODEL */
 typedef struct intModel {
     int range, kind, bytes;
 } intModel;
@@ -196,7 +210,7 @@
     goto fn_exit;
 }
 
-#ifdef HAVE_FORTRAN_BINDING
+#ifdef MPIR_HAVE_F90_MODEL
 /* The MPI standard requires that the datatypes that are returned from
    the MPI_Create_f90_xxx be both predefined and return the r (and p
    if real/complex) with which it was created.  The following contains routines
@@ -400,4 +414,4 @@
     return mpi_errno;
 }
 #endif
-#endif /* HAVE_FORTRAN_BINDING */
+#endif /* MPIR_HAVE_F90_MODEL */
